\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}
To test whether schema-induced degradation generalizes beyond the primary model, we replicate the count-curve experiment (Experiment~1) on Few-NERD using Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, a $4\times$ smaller model from a different model family. Since Llama does not reliably produce span offsets, we use text-match recall as the cross-model comparison metric.

\paragraph{Statistical estimation.}

\paragraph{Cross-model replication.}
Channel~1 replicates across model families: on Few-NERD, Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct shows a $-$43\,pp recall decline (0.702 at count${}\,=\,1$ to 0.273 at count${}\,=\,64$), compared to $-$34\,pp for Qwen3-32B (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model} in the Appendix). Per-batch recall correlation between the two models is $r = 0.725$, indicating that schema configuration drives difficulty rather than model-specific factors.

\paragraph{Summary.}
Schema size degrades extraction quality for NER and RE (Channel~1: count $\to$ recall decline), but this pattern does not hold universally. MAVEN's reversed trend demonstrates that task structure and evaluation protocol mediate the count--performance relationship. The count channel replicates on Llama-3.1-8B ($r = 0.725$), confirming model generality.

